{% extends 'site/base_public.html.twig' %}

{#
   Badges, on the shared catalogue shells.

   ⚠️ The odd one in the set, and it lands exactly where the card's design says
   it should. A badge has no availability at all — nothing to be free, busy or
   closed — so the state slot stays empty. The only fact worth leading with is
   whether YOU hold it, and that is a fact about you, which is the footer's job.
   Availability head empty, permission foot doing all the work: the same split
   as every other catalogue, just with one side unused.

   ⚠️ "Obtenu / À obtenir" is only meaningful to someone signed in. An anonymous
   visitor is not told they lack every badge — that is the same mistake the
   machines page shipped once, where `authorized == false` for a logged-out
   visitor was read as "formation requise" on machines that required none.
#}

{% block title %}{{ 'nav.badges'|trans }} - FabOS{% endblock %}

{% block stylesheets %}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('css/machines-list.css') ~ '?v=20260802-s79-events' }}">
{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
{% embed 'site/_catalogue.html.twig' with {
    route: 'app_badges',
    title: 'nav.badges'|trans,
    search: search,
    total: totalCount,
    all: allCount,
    unit: 'badge',
    unit_plural: 'badges',
    headline_count: signedIn ? ownedCount : 0,
    headline_label: 'state.held_count'|trans,
    empty_text: 'common.no_results'|trans,
} %}
    {% block cards %}
        {% for entry in cards %}
            {% set b = entry.badge %}
            {# ⚠️ `ok` (green) is safe on this card for the reason stated at the top
               of this file: a badge has no availability, so the head state slot is
               empty and there is no second green to collide with. That is the
               condition documented beside .ml-req-ok in machines-list.css — check
               it before reusing the tone on a catalogue that does draw a state chip.
               ⚠️ "À obtenir" stays neutral rather than taking the amber warning
               tone: not holding a badge is the ordinary starting position, not a
               problem to flag. Amber is reserved for the in-between state, which a
               badge does not have — you hold it or you do not. #}
            {% include 'site/_catalogue_card.html.twig' with {
                url: path('app_badge_detail', {id: b.id}),
                title: b.nom,
                meta: entry.unlocks > 0 ? entry.unlocks ~ ' ' ~ 'state.unlocks'|trans : '',
                note: b.description,
                foot: signedIn ? (entry.owned ? '✓ ' ~ 'state.held'|trans : 'state.not_held'|trans) : '',
                foot_tone: signedIn and entry.owned ? 'go' : 'muted',
                action: 'machines.see'|trans,
            } only %}
        {% endfor %}
    {% endblock %}
{% endembed %}
{% endblock %}
